The same incomplete state. The runs started at 17:44, ran for some time, but got interrupted. The runner script "block_until_ms: 300000" = 5 minutes, and it returned at the end of that period showing all FAIL. So the script's while loop didn't iterate properly.

Wait — the while loop runs while `$jobs.Values | Where-Object { -not $_.finished }` count > 0. The loop body has `Start-Sleep -Seconds 10`, then checks each process. The fact that we see NO "remaining" or "DONE" messages means the loop didn't iterate at all (or iterated zero times before exiting).

But the processes ARE running (we see partial output). So they did start.

Maybe the issue is: by the time the first iteration of the while loop starts, ALL 7 processes have already exited. But they couldn't have exited that fast — we see they wrote some prompts.

OR: the while loop is being short-circuited by some logic. Let me re-read my code:

```ps
while (($jobs.Values | Where-Object { -not $_.finished }).Count -gt 0 -and (Get-Date) -lt $deadline) {
  Start-Sleep -Seconds 10
  $remaining = 0
  foreach ($k in @($jobs.Keys)) {
    $j = $jobs[$k]
    if ($j.finished) { continue }
    $p = $j.proc
    if ($p.HasExited) {
      ...
    } else {
      $remaining++
    }
  }
  if ($remaining -gt 0) {
    Write-Host "[$(Get-Date -Format 'HH:mm:ss')] $remaining / $($jobs.Count) still running..."
  }
}
```

If all 7 have `finished = $true`, the while-condition is false and the loop exits. But how would `finished` become `$true` without the loop body running?

Hmm, unless `$p.HasExited` somehow throws, and the script ends.

Wait, `$p.HasExited` should be false for running processes. Unless `$p` is null. Then `$p.HasExited` would throw NullReferenceException.

